  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nIssued at 222 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nTemperatures remain quite warm at the start of the period, then \ntrend cooler beyond Friday. Despite rain chances being dispersed \nthrough each day of the period, there will be plenty of dry time \nbetween showers over the upcoming weekend.\n\nSurface high pressure builds southeastward from southern Canada into \nthe Great Lakes Region through the day Friday. A mid-level ridge \ncompliments the surface feature with easterly surface flow \nundercutting westerly flow aloft. High clouds will be on the \nincrease as southwest/westerly flow aloft draw moisture in around \nthe western side of the mid-level ridge. Though a stray shower \ncannot be entirely ruled out north of I-70 Friday, limited support \nand drier air below 850MB should stave off measurable precipitation.\n\nA shortwave that resides at the base of the western trough tracks \nnortheastward into the central CONUS Saturday. Ascent is broadly \nspread from the Dakotas southeastward through the Lower Mississippi \nValley, which encounter east/southeastly flow as a consequence of \nthe southeastern shift in the Great Lakes surface ridge. This will \ninitially impede moisture from reaching the surface until late \nSaturday. At that time, dry air below 700MB begins to give way to \nincreasing moisture as the surface high moves over the New England \nRegion. The system is not well organized and produces broadly \nscattered showers late in the evening into Saturday night with LREF \n75th percentile data ranging from 0.15-0.25\" from north to south. \nThe lower end of the IQR zeroes out and probabilities for measurable \nrainfall center on the 6pm-6am period Saturday night into Sunday \nmorning and peak at 30-50%. This suggests that while the area will \nsee rainfall to some degree, it will largely be light.\n\nAttention turns to two upper level waves within the amplified \nwestern trough. Long range, global guidance diverges between a \ncutoff, slow-moving closed low (GFS) and a more progressive \namplified trough (ECM) heading into next week. While this brings the \nbest potential for widespread rainfall, the evolution deserves \nattention to provide better confidence in rainfall amounts. The \nsplit flow pattern can often provide early signals for precipitation \nthat, at times, become less promising as the days progress. What is \nmore certain is the lean toward cool temperatures, though they \nremain seasonably mild. Highs through the period are generally \nin the low to mid-70s with lows in the 50s.",
